    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d871">

                <pb facs="1148" n="1138" xml:id="pg1138"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 Chase, Augustus: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-04-22T16:00:00+07:00">April 22, 1946—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 22—7:26 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>717. With reference Department’s 670, April 18, which repeated Mukden’s 26, April
                    15. Upon receipt Department’s telegram we inquired of FonOff official whether
                    there was any objection to American Consul proceeding Harbin; he felt it would
                    be preferable await Government troops Harbin, but informed us today FonOff has
                    no objection. However, according to available information, Rigg is still in
                    Changchun where he went, according to Mukden’s telegram, to make tentative
                    arrangements. If Rigg has returned Mukden and made arrangements, Embassy should
                    be informed. Please inform Mukden.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d871-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
